"Problem 1079 — Erdős Problems (https://erdosproblems.ai/wiki/problems/extremal_graph_theory/E1079)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; [BoTh81] (library home bollobas_thomason_1981_dense_neighbourhoods_turan_s_theorem, the publisher's open-archive version) carries the affirmative answer in its theorem (printed p. 111), paged at theorem_p111, in the paper's indexing with $T_r(n)$T r​(n) the $r$r-partite Turán graph and $t_r(n)$t r​(n) its number of edges: \"Let $G$G be a graph of order $n$n with $m\\ge t_r(n)$m≥t r​(n) edges. ... That external file (441 lines, toolchain Lean v4.33.0, first added on 2026-08-17) declares itself a Lean formalization of a solution to Problem 1079, names Béla Bollobás and Andrew Thomason as its informal authors and Codex and GPT-5.6 Sol as its formal authors, and proves two theorems: `erdos_problem_1079`, that for $r\\ge4$r≥4, $n\\ge2$n≥2 and at least $\\mathrm{ex}(n;K_r)$ex(n;K r​) edges some vertex of maximum degree $d$d has $n\\le2d$n≤2 d and at least $\\mathrm{ex}(d;K_{r-1})$ex(d;K r−1​) edges in its neighborhood, and `erdos_1079`, the strict form for more than $\\mathrm{ex}(n;K_r)$ex(n;K r​) edges, the target of the `formal_proof` attribute; the file carries no `sorry` and ends by printing the axioms of both theorems.\n\nErdősErdosProblemsExtremal and Structural Graph TheoryProblem 1079\n\nUpdated Oct 8\n\n# Problem 1079\n\nclaims/: The 2 claim pages of Problem 1079, one per claimant's result; the problem's standing derives from them.\n\nStatement. Let $r\\geq 4$r≥4. If $G$G is a graph on $n$n vertices with at least $\\mathrm{ex}(n;K_r)$ex(n;K r​) edges then must $G$G contain a vertex with degree $d\\gg_r n$d≫r​n whose neighbourhood contains at least $\\mathrm{ex}(d;K_{r-1})$ex(d;K r−1​) edges?\n\nFormulation. The site's wording as of 2026-09-18 (page last edited 14 October 2025). $\\mathrm{ex}(n;K_r)$ex(n;K r​) is the Turán number, the largest number of edges of a $K_r$K r​-free graph on $n$n vertices, attained by the Turán graph $T_{r-1}(n)$T r−1​(n); \"degree $d\\gg_rn$d≫r​n\" asks for $d\\ge c_rn$d≥c r​n with a constant $c_r>0$c r​>0 depending on $r$r alone; \"neighborhood\" is the set of vertices joined to the vertex, and the edges counted are those of the subgraph it induces. Erdős's 1975 wording differs in two places (Er75, p. 14): he asks it for graphs $G(n;f_r(n))$G(n;f r​(n)), where $f_r(n)$f r​(n) is the least number of edges forcing a $K_r$K r​, that is $\\mathrm{ex}(n;K_r)+1$ex(n;K r​)+1 edges, and asks the star to span at least $f_{r-1}(m)=\\mathrm{ex}(m;K_{r-1})+1$f r−1​(m)=ex(m;K r−1​)+1 edges, so that the neighborhood contains a $K_{r-1}$K r−1​ and the graph a $K_r$K r​, which is the sense of his \"If true this would be a nice generalization of Turán's theorem\"; the site's version drops both \"$+1$+1\"s. Two observations, this page's own, about the site's version. First, its conclusion is satisfied by the Turán graph $T_{r-1}(n)$T r−1​(n) itself: the neighborhood of any vertex is the union of the other $r-2$r−2 classes, whose sizes differ by at most one, so it induces the Turán graph $T_{r-2}(d)$T r−2​(d) with exactly $\\mathrm{ex}(d;K_{r-1})$ex(d;K r−1​) edges and $d\\ge(r-2)\\lfloor n/(r-1)\\rfloor$d≥(r−2)⌊n/(r−1)⌋; the exception the site makes for the Turán graph itself therefore belongs to a statement with a strict inequality somewhere, and [BoTh81] supplies it: its theorem (BoTh81, Theorem, p. 111) takes a graph with at least $\\mathrm{ex}(n;K_r)$ex(n;K r​) edges and concludes that either $G$G is the Turán graph or some vertex has at least $\\mathrm{ex}(d;K_{r-1})+1$ex(d;K r−1​)+1 edges in its neighborhood, Erdős's \"$+1$+1\", with $d>n\\bigl(1-\\frac1{r-1}-\\frac1{1+\\sqrt{r-1}}\\bigr)$d>n(1−r−1 1​−1+r−1​1​) (the paper writes $r$r for the number of parts of the Turán graph, one less than the $r$r here). [Bo83b] restates that theorem, in the same indexing, as its Theorem 1 (p. 109), with the hypothesis \"at least $t_r(n)$t r​(n) edges\" and the conclusion \"either $G\\cong T_r(n)$G≅T r​(n) or there is a vertex $v$v\" whose neighborhood induces \"more than $t_{r-1}(m)$t r−1​(m) edges\", which is the 1981 paper's \"$+1$+1\" in other words; Bondy's own Theorem 2 has \"more than\" in both places (the Status). Second, the site's label SOLVED, which the site glosses as a resolution by neither a proof nor a disproof, attaches to a yes-or-no question that the commentary answers affirmatively; the claim pages record the result as proved, and the frontmatter standing derives from them. The thread's first comment (7 October 2025) reads the intended statement as asking for a set $S$S of $\\Omega_r(n)$Ω r​(n) vertices inside some neighborhood with $\\mathrm{ex}(|S|;K_{r-1})$ex(∣S∣;K r−1​) edges, and the site's curator agreed that this was presumably Erdős's intent; the statement itself was not changed. Under that reading the answer is also yes: take $S=N(x)$S=N(x) for the vertex $x$x of the Bollobás--Thomason theorem, with $|S|=d>c_rn$∣S∣=d>c r​n and at least $\\mathrm{ex}(d;K_{r-1})+1$ex(d;K r−1​)+1 edges; for the Turán graph any neighborhood $N(x)$N(x) has exactly $\\mathrm{ex}(d;K_{r-1})$ex(d;K r−1​) edges.\n\nStatus. Solved, the site's label; the answer is yes, and the standing here is solved and proved, derived from the claim pages below. The site credits the affirmative answer, with the Turán graph as the one exception, to Bollobás and Thomason [BoTh81], credits Bondy [Bo83b] with the strengthening that for more than $\\mathrm{ex}(n;K_r)$ex(n;K r​) edges the vertex can be taken of maximum degree, and the community database records the problem as solved. The two status-defining sources are B. Bollobás and A. Thomason, \"Dense neighbourhoods and Turán's theorem\", J. Combin. Theory Ser. B 31 (1981), no. 1, 111--114, and J. A. Bondy, \"Large dense neighbourhoods and Turán's theorem\", J. Combin. Theory Ser. B 34 (1983), no. 1, 109--111 (both refereed, per their Crossref records). Neither source card holds a file. [BoTh81] (library home bollobas_thomason_1981_dense_neighbourhoods_turan_s_theorem, the publisher's open-archive version) carries the affirmative answer in its theorem (printed p. 111), paged at theorem_p111, in the paper's indexing with $T_r(n)$T r​(n) the $r$r-partite Turán graph and $t_r(n)$t r​(n) its number of edges: \"Let $G$G be a graph of order $n$n with $m\\ge t_r(n)$m≥t r​(n) edges. Then either $G=T_r(n)$G=T r​(n) or else there is a vertex $x$x such that $G[\\Gamma(x)]$G[Γ(x)], the subgraph spanned by the neighbours of $x$x, contains at least $t_{r-1}(d)+1$t r−1​(d)+1 edges, where $d=d(x)$d=d(x). Furthermore $d(x)>n(1-1/r-1/(1+\\sqrt r))$d(x)>n(1−1/r−1/(1+r​)).\" With $t_{r-1}(n)=\\mathrm{ex}(n;K_r)$t r−1​(n)=ex(n;K r​) this is the site's statement in the paper's letters, with the \"$+1$+1\" of Erdős's question in the conclusion, the Turán graph the only exception, and an explicit constant, $c_r=1-\\frac1{r-1}-\\frac1{1+\\sqrt{r-1}}$c r​=1−r−1 1​−1+r−1​1​ in the site's indexing ($c_4\\approx0.30$c 4​≈0.30). The paper attributes the conjecture to [Er75], its [2], in the form \"if $e(G^n)>t_r(n)$e(G n)>t r​(n)\" (p. 111). Its proof (pp. 112--114) is followed on the card, which records four observations (three misprints and a final inequality that needs $n\\ge r(1+\\sqrt r)^2/2$n≥r(1+r​)2/2 in the paper's $r$r). [Bo83b] (library home bondy_1983_large_dense_neighbourhoods_turan_s_theorem, the publisher's version with its erratum) carries Bondy's own strengthening as its Theorem 2 (p. 110), paged at theorem_2, in the same indexing: \"Let $G$G be a simple graph on $n$n vertices and more than $t_r(n)$t r​(n) edges, where $r\\ge2$r≥2, and let $v$v be a vertex in $G$G of degree $m=\\Delta(G)$m=Δ(G). Source. erdosproblems.com/1079, accessed 2026-09-18: the problem page (SOLVED, glossed by the site as a resolution by neither a proof nor a disproof; last edited 14 October 2025; source keys [Bo83b], [BoTh81], [Er75]; commentary quoting [Er75] and citing [BoTh81] and [Bo83b]), its three-comment discussion thread (7 and 13 October 2025) and its empty proof-claim tab. Cite as: T. F. Bloom, Erdős Problem #1079, https://www.erdosproblems.com/1079, accessed 2026-09-18.\n\nReferences.\n\n  * [BoTh81] Bollobás, Béla and Thomason, Andrew, Dense neighbourhoods and Turán's theorem. J. Combin. Theory Ser. B 31 (1981), no. 1, 111--114, doi:10.1016/S0095-8956(81)80016-0 (issued August 1981; the Crossref record carries the publisher's open-archive license and no abstract); the Theorem and the introduction, printed p. 111; the proof, printed pp. 112--114. Library home: bollobas_thomason_1981_dense_neighbourhoods_turan_s_theorem (the publisher's open-archive version; no file is held); the theorem is paged at theorem_p111.\n## Current assessment\n\nThe question (site formulation of 2026-09-18). The statement above; SOLVED; last edited 14 October 2025. The commentary, in the corpus's words: it repeats Erdős's remark that a positive answer would generalize Turán's theorem, states that the answer is yes unless $G$G is the Turán graph itself, proved by Bollobás and Thomason [BoTh81], and that Bondy [Bo83b] showed the vertex can be taken of maximum degree when $G$G has more than $\\mathrm{ex}(n;K_r)$ex(n;K r​) edges. The thread: a comment of 7 October 2025 (the account zach hunter) on the intended reading (a neighborhood set $S$S of size $\\Omega_r(n)$Ω r​(n) with $\\mathrm{ex}(|S|;K_{r-1})$ex(∣S∣;K r−1​) edges) and the site author's agreement the same day; a comment of 13 October 2025 (the account msawhney) giving the two publishers' article pages with their journal issues (JCTB August 1981, vol. 31, issue 1; JCTB February 1983, vol. 34, issue 1), restating Bondy's strengthening for graphs with $\\mathrm{ex}(n;K_r)+1$ex(n;K r​)+1 edges, and saying the reference was located by GPT-5 Pro. The proof-claim tab is empty. The community database record says solved (14 October 2025).\n\nStatus support. The support for the site's label is of two kinds, and the first